    {
      "id": "82RROZwM26umpOZFcK3WX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 91.45258692502239,
      "y": 1091.7920787417318,
      "width": 632.9803149808869,
      "height": 975,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 816892202,
      "version": 1417,
      "versionNonce": 2112493814,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794973230163,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Singleton Bean\n-------------------\n\nWhen we define bean with singleton scope then :\n\n-> container creates a single instance of that bean .\n\n-> all request for that bean using getBean() will return the\nsame object , which is cached .\n\n-> singleton is the default value of scope if no other value is\nspecified .\n\n-> Modification through any object ref will be reflected for all\nother object reference to the bean .\n\n\nThe Prototype Scope\n====================\n\nWhen we define  a bean with prototype scope then :\n\n-> The container creates a new instance of that bean every\ntime a request is made to that bean .\n\n->It means that whenever we call getBeans() or we inject that\nbean into another bean the constructor will create new object\nand return it .\n\n\nPrototype bean do not share their state (data) with other\nbean instances . Each instance has its own independent state\nand any modification to one bean instance will not affect other\ninstances .\n\nSpring does not manage the complete lifecycle of prototype\nbean . The conatainer instantiate the and configure prototype\nbean and hands it over to the client , with no further record of\nthat prototype instance .",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Singleton Bean\n-------------------\n\nWhen we define bean with singleton scope then :\n\n-> container creates a single instance of that bean .\n\n-> all request for that bean using getBean() will return the same object , which is cached .\n\n-> singleton is the default value of scope if no other value is specified .\n\n-> Modification through any object ref will be reflected for all other object reference to the bean .\n\n\nThe Prototype Scope\n====================\n\nWhen we define  a bean with prototype scope then :\n\n-> The container creates a new instance of that bean every time a request is made to that bean .\n\n->It means that whenever we call getBeans() or we inject that bean into another bean the constructor will create new object and return it .\n\n\nPrototype bean do not share their state (data) with other bean instances . Each instance has its own independent state and any modification to one bean instance will not affect other instances .\n\nSpring does not manage the complete lifecycle of prototype bean . The conatainer instantiate the and configure prototype bean and hands it over to the client , with no further record of that prototype instance .",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
